# Six, investment arguments

# Look at the core logic (3 pieces)

1. ** The structural substitution trend for fibre cement is the most definitive growth in the long term**: fibre cement, with its durability, fire protection and beauty, is continuously taking its share of the ethylene (about 30% of the U.S. outer wall market) and timber. This trend has been ongoing for decades. James Hardie is the largest beneficiary of this trend, with 90% of the share of the product group. Periodic fluctuations in the housing market will not change this structural direction.

2. ** The market ' s double panic over AZEK acquisition and housing cycle has been overpricing**: the higher stock price fell by about 36%, with nine analysts unanimously rating "strong buy-in". The early synergies of AZEK were more than anticipated (new distributors and distributors committed to meet) and would have increased significantly the profits if the cost-consilience target of $125 million had been met. The current valuation (based on normalization earnings of about 15 x) fully reflects pessimistic scenarios.

3. ** The housing market will eventually recover, with cyclical low-point offers of buy-in**: The ageing of housing stock in the United States (over 40 years of median housing age) creates a continuing demand for renovation. Once interest rates fall, depressed demand for new construction and renovation will be released. Management expects that the 2027 sales will begin to recover. For patient long-term investors, buying cyclical poles at low-cycle levels is a classic investment opportunity for value investment.

# Look out core logic (3 bars)

1. **AZEK acquisitions may be a costly mistake**: morning stars themselves evaluate the trade as potentially "value-depreciating". James Hardie paid $8.75 billion high prices for a narrower, less profitable business in the mound. The complex market for materials is much more competitive and less profitable than fibre cement. If the integration or synergism is not as good as expected, companies will bear large debts without corresponding profit growth. Net indebtedness of $4.5 billion is a heavy burden.

2. **The housing market recovery schedule is highly uncertain**: housing affordability is at its lowest level in decades in the current high interest rate environment. There is great uncertainty about when interest rates will fall, how much will fall, and when data on housing start-ups will be transmitted. If the high interest rate environment continues to be FY 2028 or more, companies will experience a long period of decline under high leverage.

3. ** ** The sharp contraction of free cash flows weakens investor confidence**: FY 2026 free cash flows guide is only "at least $200 million", down by about 47% from FY 2025 million. In critical times when leverage is needed, cash flows shrink considerably. If the recovery of FY 2027 is not as good as expected, companies may have to leverage through other means (emitting more, cutting capital spending, reducing shareholder returns) to dilute the value of existing shareholders.

